# Server-only (SSOT-03: src/config/env.server.ts)
# The Java service answers every read and write this storefront makes. There is
# no stand-in: with nothing listening here the store renders its error states.
JAVA_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080
JAVA_API_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# F-01 — signs the session cookie (HMAC-SHA256). REQUIRED, at least 32 characters:
# the app refuses to start without it, because with no secret a cookie somebody
# wrote by hand could not be told from a customer's. Generate one with
#   node -e "console.log(require('node:crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
# and use a different value in every environment. Set SESSION_SECRET_PREVIOUS to
# the OLD value only while rotating (tokens signed with it still open; nothing
# new is signed with it), then remove it after seven days.
SESSION_SECRET=
# SESSION_SECRET_PREVIOUS=

# F-02 — the Cloudflare Access service token this server presents to Java, which
# sits behind Access and admits no other caller (plan TD-1). Leave both unset for
# local development, where nothing stands in front of the backend. REQUIRED, both
# together, wherever Vercel sets VERCEL_ENV to preview or production: the app
# refuses to start without them. Use a different token for staging and production.
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID=
# CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_SECRET=

# F-08 — the Sentry project's DSN for server-side error tracking (plan TD-11).
# OPTIONAL: unset, nothing is sent. Server-side only; no Sentry code ships to the
# browser, which reports through POST /api/client-error.
# SENTRY_DSN=

# F-06 — the shared secret of POST /api/revalidate, the route the Java worker calls
# after an import so a withdrawn or changed product leaves the cache at once. At
# least 32 characters, the SAME value as the backend's
# ALASAD_STOREFRONT_REVALIDATE_SECRET (and the backend's
# ALASAD_STOREFRONT_REVALIDATE_URL is https://<store domain>/api/revalidate).
# OPTIONAL: unset, the route answers 401 to everyone (closed), which is right for
# local development. Set it in Production and Preview on Vercel.
# REVALIDATE_SECRET=

# Client-safe (SSOT-03: src/config/env.client.ts). SEC-01: treat as published.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# R-03 — the CDN host that serves product photographs, a BARE host with no scheme
# (plan 3.4.4; production value media.<store domain>). OPTIONAL: unset, the
# fixture images in public/ are used and the Content-Security-Policy gains no
# media origin. Set, photographs and the hero film load from
# https://<host>, `next/image` picks the 480, 960 or 1600 wide file itself, and
# Try-On fetches the garment from it (R-04). It is baked in at BUILD time, so
# changing it needs a redeploy. The backend's ALASAD_MEDIA_BASE_URL must name the
# same host or the browser will be refused by the policy.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_HOST=

# Dev only, and OPTIONAL. Hosts allowed to load Next's /_next/* resources
# cross-origin, so the store can be opened from a phone on the same network.
# Next blocks these by default: without it the page renders but NO client
# JavaScript runs, so links work and search, the bag and checkout do not.
# Comma-separated bare hosts, no scheme or port. Leave unset on a machine that
# only ever serves localhost.
# DEV_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=192.168.1.50

# Architecture §24 Try-On, and OPTIONAL. Read by src/features/try-on, which
# calls the image model from the Next server; neither value reaches the
# browser (SEC-01).
#
# The key is the ONLY thing standing between this checkout and a real
# generation: `imageModelProvider` in src/features/try-on/api/gemini-provider.ts
# posts the customer's photograph and the garment's to Google's
# generativelanguage endpoint. With no key the feature reports itself
# unavailable (§28.5). The try-on button is drawn either way; ProductTryOn.tsx
# explains why it is never hidden.
#
# Generations are metered and billed by the provider, so treat the key as a
# cost as well as a credential. SEC-10: it belongs in .env.local, which is
# gitignored, and in the host's own environment settings — never in committed
# code, and never pasted into a chat transcript.
# TRY_ON_PROVIDER_API_KEY=
# TRY_ON_PROVIDER_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-image

# Read only by `node scripts/smoke.mjs <url>` (the post-deploy smoke check), as an
# alternative to passing the address on the command line. Not read by the app.
# SMOKE_URL=https://<store domain>

# Deployment (plan section 21.2). Set in the Vercel project, not in this file. The
# complete table (every variable, where it is set, example, secret or not) is
# DEPLOYMENT-ENV.md at the MVP root:
#
#   JAVA_API_BASE_URL               https://api.<store domain>  (preview: the staging API)
#   CF_ACCESS_CLIENT_ID / _SECRET   token `storefront-prod`     (preview: `storefront-staging`)
#   SESSION_SECRET                  a different value per environment
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL             https://<store domain>      (preview: the preview alias)
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_HOST          media.<store domain>
#   SENTRY_DSN                      the storefront project's DSN
#   TRY_ON_PROVIDER_API_KEY         production only, the billed key
#   REVALIDATE_SECRET               shared with the backend's cache-refresh secret
#                                   (read by plan F-06: POST /api/revalidate)
#
# The function region is fixed in vercel.json (bom1, Mumbai). The GitHub
# repository variable STORE_URL (https://<store domain>) is what the post-deploy
# smoke check (scripts/smoke.mjs) is run against.
